   "## Before AD 37",
   "Gildas was a monk of the Britons, taught, his Life says, at Illtud’s school at Llanilltud Fawr, and he wrote his great book, The Ruin of Britain, in about the 540s. He had read the old histories of Rome and the books of the church, and when he came to tell how the faith reached his own people he gave a date: the latter part of the reign of Tiberius Caesar. Tiberius ruled from AD 14 until his death in March AD 37. Christ was crucified in about AD 30 or 33. By Gildas’s reckoning, then, the faith of Christ had reached the Island of Britain within a few years of the Resurrection.",
   "Gildas did not write it as a guess. He wrote it ‘as we know’, as something his readers knew already. Britain lay at the edge of the known world, but it was not cut off. Tin, lead, corn, hunting dogs and slaves went out from the island to the Roman world, and merchants, soldiers and travellers came in. A new faith that spread along the roads and sea lanes of the empire could reach the Britons as quickly as it reached many places nearer Rome.",
   "## Three traditions of the first coming",
   "Wales and Britain kept three great traditions of how the faith first came, and each has its own long read on this site.",
   "The first is Joseph of Arimathea, the rich man who laid the body of Christ in his own tomb. Glastonbury tradition holds that he came to Britain in AD 63 with twelve companions, that a British king gave them the island of Ynys Wydrin, and that they built there the first church in Britain, of wattle, in honour of Mary.",
   "The second is Brân Fendigaid, Brân the Blessed. In the Mabinogi he is the giant king of the Island of the Mighty. In later Welsh tradition he is the father of Caradog, the king who fought Rome, who went with his son to Rome as a hostage, spent seven years there, and came home bringing the faith of Christ to the Cymry. That, the tradition says, is why he is called Bendigaid, the Blessed.",
   "The third is King Lucius, in Welsh Lleurwg, Lles ap Coel, or Lleufer Mawr, the Great Light. The Book of the Popes, written in Rome in the 500s, records that he wrote to Pope Eleutherius asking to be made a Christian, and Bede and the Welsh historians tell how teachers came and how the Britons took the faith as a people.",
   "## Britons in Rome",
   "In AD 51 Caradog, Caratacus to the Romans, king of the Britons, who had fought the legions for eight years from the hills of the Silures and the Ordovices, was handed over to Rome and taken there in chains with his wife, his daughter and his brothers. Before the Emperor Claudius he spoke so nobly that Claudius pardoned them all. Caradog’s family lived on in Rome, in the very years when Saint Paul was writing his letters and the first Christians of the city were meeting in one another’s houses.",
   "Rome itself has left hints that Britons were among them. The poet Martial, writing later in the first century, praises a lady named Claudia Rufina, ‘born of the blue-painted Britons’, and in another poem he celebrates the marriage of a Claudia to his friend Pudens. And in Saint Paul’s second letter to Timothy, written from Rome, Paul sends greetings from ‘Pudens, and Linus, and Claudia, and all the brethren’. Linus, Irenaeus tells us, became the first bishop of Rome after Peter. Welsh tradition, set out by the Welsh priest Richard Williams Morgan in St Paul in Britain (1861), holds that Claudia was Gwladys, Caradog’s daughter, and Linus her brother, so that a son of a British king led the church of Rome. There is more on Caradog and his family on his page.",
   "There were others. Tacitus tells that in AD 57 Pomponia Graecina, wife of Aulus Plautius, the general who led the Roman invasion of Britain, was accused of following a ‘foreign superstition’, and many have read that as the faith of Christ. Clement of Rome, writing about AD 96, says that Saint Paul preached ‘to the limits of the West’, and Welsh tradition holds that Paul himself came to Britain. In the fourth century Eusebius, the first historian of the church, wrote that some of the apostles had crossed the Ocean to the islands called British.",
   "## The witnesses",
   "By about AD 200 the Christians of Carthage in North Africa knew of the Britons. Tertullian, their great writer, listed the peoples who had come to believe in Christ, and among them he named the Britons, and not only those under Rome. The faith had gone beyond the frontier, into the lands of free Britons that the legions had never conquered.",
   "> The haunts of the Britons, inaccessible to the Romans, but subjugated to Christ. | Tertullian, An Answer to the Jews, chapter 7, translated by S. Thelwall (1870)",
   "Some forty years later Origen of Alexandria, the greatest Christian scholar of his age, wrote that the power of Christ was with those in Britain, cut off from the rest of the world, and asked when, before Christ came, the land of Britain had ever agreed in the worship of one God. From both ends of the Roman world, the witnesses say the same thing: the Britons had the faith.",
   "## The martyrs",
   "Then came the persecutions, when Christians across the empire were hunted for refusing to sacrifice to the gods. Gildas names the martyrs of Britain who would not give up their faith: Alban of Verulamium, and Julius and Aaron, citizens of the City of the Legions, Caerleon on the Usk, in the heart of what is now Wales. Bede tells how Alban sheltered a priest in his house, took the priest’s cloak and gave himself up in his place. Julius and Aaron are the first Christians of Wales whose names we know, and churches stood at Caerleon in their memory for centuries.",
   "## Bishops of Britain at Arles, 314",
   "In 313 the Emperor Constantine, who had been proclaimed emperor by the army at York, made the faith free throughout the empire. The very next year he called the bishops of the West to a council at Arles in Gaul, and the church of Britain was there. Three bishops came from the island: Eborius of York, Restitutus of London, and Adelfius, whose city the copyists garbled. The Victorian scholars Haddan and Stubbs read it as Caerleon. A priest and a deacon came with them.",
   "That one fact says a great deal. A year after Christians were allowed to worship openly, Britain could send bishops of three cities across the sea to sit with the bishops of Gaul, Italy and Spain. A church does not grow bishops overnight. The British church of 314 was already old.",
   "The ground has kept its signs too: the silver vessels of a Christian congregation buried at Water Newton, the chi-rho, the first letters of Christ’s name in Greek, painted on the walls of the villa at Lullingstone, and the face of Christ in a mosaic floor at Hinton St Mary, one of the earliest pictures of him in Britain.",
   "## Pelagius and Patrick",
   "Around 400 two Britons made the faith of their island known to the world. Pelagius, a Briton who went to Rome, became one of the most famous thinkers of the age. He taught that God made every person free to choose good, and he argued it with Augustine of Hippo himself. His books are the oldest surviving works by any writer from Britain.",
   "At the same time a boy named Patrick was growing up in a Christian family of the Britons. In his own Confession he tells us that his father Calpurnius was a deacon and his grandfather Potitus a priest. Three generations of churchmen in one British family: the faith had long been at home in the island. Taken by Irish raiders as a young man, Patrick escaped, came home, and went back to Ireland of his own will to make it Christian. The apostle of Ireland was a Briton.",
   "## The church of the saints",
   "When the legions left, the faith did not leave with them. In the Age of the Saints, from the 400s to the 600s, Wales became a land of llannau, the enclosed churches whose names are still on the map. Dyfrig, Illtud, Dewi, Teilo, Cadog, Padarn, Beuno and Gwenfrewi founded churches and schools. Illtud’s school at Llanilltud Fawr taught Samson, who carried the faith to Brittany, and Gildas himself. The saints of Wales went to Cornwall, Brittany and Ireland, and the church of the Britons was one of the brightest in Christendom.",
   "## Augustine’s Oak",
   "In 597 a monk named Augustine landed in Kent, sent by Pope Gregory the Great to convert the English, who were still pagans. By Gildas’s reckoning the church of the Britons was by then more than five hundred and fifty years old. It had its own bishops, its own monasteries and its own ways, and it had come through Roman persecution and the English wars with its faith whole.",
   "In about 602 or 603 Augustine called the bishops of the Britons to meet him at a place on the border of the West Saxons that Bede says was still called Augustine’s Oak. He asked them to accept his authority, to keep Easter the Roman way, and to join him in preaching to the English. At a second meeting came seven British bishops and many learned men, most of them from the great monastery of Bangor-is-y-Coed, where, Bede says, there were so many monks that they were divided into seven companies of three hundred, under their abbot Dunod.",
   "On the way they asked a holy hermit what they should do. He told them: if Augustine rises to meet you, he is a humble servant of Christ, and you may listen to him; if he stays seated, he despises you, and you should not. Augustine stayed in his seat.",
   "> They answered they would do none of those things, nor receive him as their archbishop. | Bede, Ecclesiastical History of the English People, Book II, chapter 2, translated by A. M. Sellar (1907)",
   "The bishops of the Britons stood firm. Their church was not Augustine’s to command. It was older than his mission and older than the English kingdoms, and they kept their own ways. When, a few years later, the king of Northumbria cut down the monks of Bangor-is-y-Coed at the battle of Chester as they prayed for their people, they died as the faithful of an ancient church.",
   "The church of the Britons went its own way for more than a century and a half. Only in 768, the Welsh annals record, did Elfoddw, ‘man of God’ and bishop in Gwynedd, bring it to keep Easter on the Roman day. Even then the bishops of St Davids held for centuries that their church owed nothing to Canterbury, and Gerallt Gymro fought for it to the Pope himself.",
   "## When was the British church founded?",
   "No charter survives, because no church of the first century kept one. But the witnesses line up across the centuries. Gildas sets the coming of the faith under Tiberius, before AD 37. The traditions of Wales and Glastonbury bring Joseph, Brân and the family of Caradog into the first century. Rome’s own Book of the Popes has a king of Britain asking for baptism in the second. Tertullian names the Christian Britons by 200, the martyrs died at Caerleon and Verulamium, and in 314 the bishops of Britain sat in council at Arles.",
   "The faith of the Cymry is the oldest unbroken Christian tradition in the Island of Britain. When Augustine came to Kent, the church of the Britons had been at prayer for more than five centuries. Dewi’s last words to his people were to keep the faith. The Britons had been keeping it since the days of Tiberius.",
